WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:02.051
(bright music)
- Woo-hoo!

00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:03.896
Take me, gravity!

00:00:03.896 --> 00:00:06.037
(water splashes)
- Woo-hoo!

00:00:06.037 --> 00:00:07.680
(water splashes)

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.513
- Yeah!

00:00:10.155 --> 00:00:11.654
Ah-ha!

00:00:11.654 --> 00:00:15.921
- Woo! (laughs)

00:00:17.314 --> 00:00:20.410
- [Enrico] "Luca" is the
story of a young sea monster

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.710
who really wants to be a human boy,

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.060
and it's about him and
his best friend Alberto

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.463
navigating an adventure
into the human world.

00:00:29.945 --> 00:00:31.401
- Wow.

00:00:31.401 --> 00:00:32.867
Uh-oh.
(boy whimpers)

00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:35.900
- Hello, my name is Enrico Casarosa,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.180
and I am the director of
Disney and Pixar's "Luca."

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.110
Here we are in a scene
toward the end of act two.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.070
The idea here is that
our two protagonists,

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.420
Luca and Alberto,

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.450
are starting to really
go their separate way

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.070
and starting to have conflict.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.980
Giulia is of course becoming the person

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.340
that is in between them.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.800
Luca is learning a lot more from her

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.327
about this human world,

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:05.160
Alberto doesn't really know
about the human world that well

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.930
so he's starting to feel
more and more jealous.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.700
So when we started writing this scene

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.490
we wanted already to kind of

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.610
twist a little bit of
a knife into Alberto,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.240
so Luca asks about the train.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.160
This idea of going to school with Giulia

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.720
is of course the antithesis

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.010
of everything that Alberto wants.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.700
Alberto wants to keep his friend,

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.770
so he's very afraid.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.430
And so at the beginning here

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.630
it was all about showing how
Alberto is really annoyed

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.020
at Luca's interest, at
anything that Giulia does.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.070
Of course this pushes
Alberto to do something

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.135
that's very dangerous.

00:01:40.135 --> 00:01:41.390
- Then what does he need?
- Me.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.347
We'll just ride it like we
did on the island, together.

00:01:44.347 --> 00:01:45.650
Andiamo.
- No!

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.140
- This scene is not only mirroring

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.280
the first time they do this on the island,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.650
but it's also exactly the same set

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.850
where a few scenes later we're gonna have

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.340
the climax of the movie,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.100
so it really maps out Luca's change.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.310
He's very scared and he needs Alberto

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.630
to lead him down the first time.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.080
Here he's still struggling with it

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.337
and Alberto kind of takes
him down the wrong way,

00:02:08.337 --> 00:02:09.850
in a dangerous way,

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.700
and ultimately in the third act

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.100
right in this party

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.550
not only will lead down without fear

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.440
but it is gonna save Alberto,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.610
and on top of that even be willing to show

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.604
his sea monster self.

00:02:21.604 --> 00:02:24.437
(uplifting music)

00:02:26.495 --> 00:02:29.530
And the really fun part of this crazy ride

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:30.990
was seeing the town.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.830
We knew that the town had to be alive,

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.420
the town is a whole character,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.110
and we looked for opportunities

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.270
to bring the specifics
of a small Italian town.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.450
The old man playing chess.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.590
Between story artists that come up

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.090
with these wonderful gags

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.280
and then animators that even add something

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.940
to the last minute,

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.540
for example the lemons
that end up in their faces.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.340
That's not something that
happens right away on the page

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.260
or even in storyboards,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.100
sometimes it really
happens as we start working

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.640
with the animators.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.470
And then I love that little alley

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.550
they go through at the end

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.960
because it just felt like such a portal,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.390
and it's very, very typical.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.500
If you go to Cinque Terre

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.690
you're gonna totally feel
like you've been already

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.507
if you've watched "Luca"
a couple of times.

00:03:11.507 --> 00:03:14.183
(boys screaming)

00:03:14.183 --> 00:03:15.504
(water splashes)
(Ercole gasps)

00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:17.550
- Porca palette! What was that?

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.070
- It was important to set up Ercole

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.260
hearing something and activating,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.070
that was the goal of that moment

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.670
that we were trying to
figure out how to best do it,

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.070
because he couldn't see
them but he had to really,

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.070
you had to buy that a
couple of minutes later

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.150
he's gonna show up 'cause
he's looking around.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.230
And of course the fun of
Ciccio offering a focaccia.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.460
We joke in the whole movie

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.610
nobody eats a focaccia sandwich,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:44.130
it's like every single
sandwich Ercole is around

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.020
gets kicked away,

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.890
or he's about to bite
it and he puts it away.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:48.820
But then of course, yeah,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.760
this dangerous moment is
like, oh, Giulia could see us,

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.960
and that's when we're starting
to build Luca's frustration.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.460
Interestingly, this shot
was very, very difficult

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.070
because it just felt like Giulia

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.950
would have seen them very clearly,

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.520
so this shot was full of little cheats

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.300
and I think that we had to move Giulia

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.240
quite a bit further away
from where she was literally.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.753
- She'll see us, come on.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.600
- [Enrico] This is the
beginning of the scene,

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.120
we used to call it splash.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.873
It's really where things
are coming to a head.

00:04:18.873 --> 00:04:21.010
- You crashed into the sea.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.130
- [Alberto] It's fine.
- Nothing's fine.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.560
- All of the color palette

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.480
was so much fun to really
think deeply about.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.290
We'd do a whole color
script for the whole movie

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.860
that maps around emotions
of different scenes.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.720
We were quite careful to not
set that many scenes at sunset

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.100
so that this created a
contrast and a sense of drama.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.980
We knew that it would
be supported wonderfully

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:48.810
by very, very saturated
yellows and oranges.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.770
The waves are a wonderful
little thing we did here

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.460
because we never did waves like that

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.780
anywhere else in the movie.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.220
You will notice almost
every single other scene

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.560
has pretty common waves.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.690
Here, we allowed the effects team

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.430
to just give us turmoil, you know?

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:06.270
We wanted of course to
feel even the nature

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.923
and the weather is feeling the conflict.

00:05:09.923 --> 00:05:13.230
- Shut up!
(boys grunt)

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.170
- We had a lot of discussions
with our story team

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:17.410
that from day one they've,
this is a kid's movie,

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.240
they will have to go
to blows at some point,

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.700
they should have a fight.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.360
I love that bite, by the way,

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.710
because it was a hint of
comedy in the middle of pathos.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:30.634
We had long discussions about
whether it should be dramatic

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:33.530
or also a little bit playful and goofy,

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:37.512
and I really wanted those
two things side by side.

00:05:37.512 --> 00:05:38.920
(boys grunt)

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.660
For a long time,

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.860
Alberto wasn't going to
be about to take a swing,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.240
and I remember Pete Docter
had this wonderful suggestion,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.140
he was like, "Tell me the
fight is gonna continue."

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.080
And so we kinda made a little
adjustment to this animation,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.470
like he's gonna really punch him,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.283
which I always thought
is such a wonderful note.

00:05:57.393 --> 00:06:00.200
(waves whooshing)

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.520
This shot is actually mirrored

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.700
in our finale, in our goodbye.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.790
There's a very similar shot
where she hugs both of them

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.123
at the train station.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:11.720
I always loved this moment,

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.470
the way they look at
each other divided by her

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.420
is so wonderful.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.220
In this version of the scene, of course,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.800
Alberto is gonna really
reveal their biggest secret,

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.349
so it's really betraying
Luca in many ways.

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:31.152
(Giulia whimpers)
(dramatic music)

00:06:31.152 --> 00:06:32.240
- [Giulia] Help!

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.520
- I've always been very proud of the fact

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.900
that this feels like a sudden choice,

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.110
but even more so the choice of Luca

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.620
to disown him.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.056
This is Luca denying himself.
- I knew this would happen.

00:06:44.056 --> 00:06:45.870
- Sea monster!
(dramatic music)

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.007
- Did you hear that? This way!

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:52.180
- And of course we wanted him
to even twist the knife in.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:53.370
You know, what have I done?

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:56.220
We wanted to feel the
emotion of Luca like, (gasps)

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.500
oh my gosh, I caused this,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.803
and I almost got my friend killed.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:04.820
Interestingly, we didn't
immediately script it that way.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.120
We had scripted it in a way

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.150
where Alberto was dragging
Luca in and betraying him

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:15.000
and of course Giulia would
know both of their secrets.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.640
And we realized that that kind of put Luca

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.250
very mad at Alberto

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.480
but it actually made our protagonist

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:22.920
a little bit non-active.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.100
He was more of a victim.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:28.030
As soon as we thought of
actually allowing Luca

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.263
to do worse than Alberto,

00:07:30.263 --> 00:07:33.880
I think it activated this
surprise, first of all,

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.110
'cause I think we were
used to the other way,

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:39.120
so I think we didn't see it coming

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.380
and I think a lot of people feel this

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.360
as a surprising moment.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.800
And allowing him to do the wrong thing.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.730
We even had a few people kind
of doubt that this could work

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.470
because it was so callous
of Luca to do this,

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:52.960
but now we could make it up to Alberto.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.040
You would have to try,

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.280
act three was really all about
trying to get Alberto back.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.860
- What are you doing?

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.378
- I'm okay, I'll be back tomorrow.

00:08:01.378 --> 00:08:03.280
I'm gonna fix this!

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:08.800
- It's really strange and
wonderful to watch this scene

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.290
after so many months.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.730
What I love the most about
it is that it's surprising,

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.490
and that's an important
thing making these movies.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:17.470
Audiences are so sophisticated

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.610
and I hope it does surprise the audiences

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.650
when they see this moment of betrayal.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.210
And again, hopefully
then give us the juice

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.740
to really want to see Luca succeed

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.884
in winning back Alberto in act three.

00:08:31.884 --> 00:08:34.467
(gentle music)

